    <text>
        <body>
            <div type="edition" xml:space="preserve" xml:lang="la" resp="#AA">
                <ab>
                    <lb n="1"/><persName type="attested"><name>Aemiliae</name>
                    <lb n="2"/><persName type="patronym"><name><expan><abbr>C</abbr><ex>ai</ex></expan></name> <g type="interpunct">·</g> <expan><abbr>f</abbr><ex>iliae</ex></expan></persName>
                    <lb n="3"/><name>Aequae</name></persName>
                    <lb n="4"/><expan><abbr>sacerd</abbr><ex>oti</ex></expan> <g type="interpunct">·</g> <persName type="divine"><name>divae</name>
                    <lb n="5"/><name>Plotinae</name></persName>
                    <lb n="6"/><expan><abbr>colleg</abbr><ex>ium</ex></expan> <g type="interpunct">·</g> <expan><abbr>cent</abbr><ex>onariorum</ex></expan>
                    <lb n="7"/>titulo usa
                </ab>
            </div>
            <div type="apparatus">
                <listApp>
                    <app><note>Text from autopsy</note></app>
                </listApp>
            </div>
            <div type="translation" xml:lang="en" resp="#AA">
                <p>
                    To Aemilia Aequa, daughter of Gaius, sacerdos of the divine Plotina. The college of textile workers made use of this inscription.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div type="commentary">
                <p>Garzetti (1991)  postulates that <ref target="EmpWor000691.xml">EmpWor691</ref> was likely in the same assemblage as this one, as both were dedicated by the same college, and the P. Clodius Sura of this inscription could be a relation to the Clodia Secunda of that inscription (either her father or brother).</p> 
            </div>
